--TEST--
`Core\Router::url` and `Core\Router::urlAbsolute` throw on a name no route declares, and refuse exactly the same names as each other — `rule:routing/matching-is-not-dispatching`, `rule:routing/an-absolute-link-takes-a-configured-origin`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The depth claim is *agreement*: one question asked of the two members that
// share § 4's lookup, asserting that they answer the same rather than what
// each answered. `urlAbsolute` is `url` with `rule:routing/an-absolute-link-takes-a-configured-origin`'s configured origin
// in front, so a name one of them resolves and the other does not would mean
// two lookups had grown apart — which is exactly what a second copy of the
// table would produce.
//
// The names are held in a variable rather than written at the call, because
// § 4 makes a *literal* name that is not a declared route a compile error and
// a computed one a throw. This case is about the throw, and it stays about the
// throw once the compile-time table lands.
array<string> $names = [
    // The spelling `examples/routes.nvs` uses, which is a real route there and
    // no route here: this program declares no `#[Route]` and no `autoload`
    // root, so the table it asks is empty by construction (`rule:routing/table-is-opt-in` — a
    // program that declares no route still performs no scan).
    "Users::show",
    // The empty name, which no route may carry: § 1's `name` is optional and
    // never derived, so there is no route whose name is nothing.
    "",
    // A near miss on the first, which is the typo the launderer exists to
    // catch before it reaches a link.
    "users.show",
];

var $agree = 0;
foreach ($names as string $n) {
    var $path = "resolved";
    try {
        Core\Router::url($n, []);
    } catch (RuntimeError $e) {
        $path = "threw";
    }

    var $absolute = "resolved";
    try {
        Core\Router::urlAbsolute($n, []);
    } catch (RuntimeError $e) {
        $absolute = "threw";
    }

    if ($path == $absolute) {
        $agree = $agree + 1;
    }
}

// Both halves: the two members agreed on every name, and what they agreed on
// is the refusal. Counting the agreements rather than printing each answer is
// what makes a member that grew its own lookup fail here.
echo "agree=", $agree as string, "/", Core\Arr::count($names) as string, "\n";

var $refused = 0;
foreach ($names as string $n) {
    try {
        Core\Router::url($n, []);
    } catch (RuntimeError $e) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
    }
}
echo "refused=", $refused as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
agree=3/3
refused=3
